Date: Wed, 22 Sep 93 20:01:06 -0700
From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Subject: PHIL: Recovering Objectivist

   > "Kids, don't try this at home!" :-)
   .....
   >Any Objectivism deprogrammers out there :-) ?

   I've been reading these anti-Objectivisim comments (in this group,
   that is) for a while. Frankly, I think the "dangers" of Objectivism
   are being over-stated.

   It's a lot of fun to poke fun at Rand and some of her screwy
   idiosyncracies 

I am not in the least bit poking fun at Rand.  I take her philosophy
very seriously -- I was trying to lighten up with the smileys, but
didn't intend to give anyone the impression that the subject matter
itself was to be taken lightly.  I concede happily that Objectivism
may be the best philosophy for some people -- I just don't think I'm
one of them.  Gad -- I feel like I'm trying to justify a sex-change
operation or something!

   Most of us skipped large sections of John Galt's speech and mainly
   took from Rand a general notion that "we are not our brother's
   keeper," that in fact such a philosophy of self-esteem and
   self-interest is in fact a "good" thing.

< 1/2:-) >  I, on the other hand, read the entire speech without a
break, sotto voce because I was in public, and it took almost exactly
as long as Rand said it would take (three hours, I think it was).  I
loved it and was very moved by it, because it articulated clearly and
powerfully (to my possibly less-than-critical ear) exactly what I had
always unconsciously accepted.

   The talk of "recovering Objectivists" is cute, and fun, but is mostly
   just a joke. 

Maybe for most people, but not for me.  I'm working hard at this, and
it's taking up a lot of my time and mental energy.  

To those for whom it is a joke, I can only say that I look forward to
the day when I can look back and find it funny.

   I put it on a par with Heinlein (like most of us, I'd read
   all 40 or so novels he wrote by the time I was in the 9th grade).

For any statistics hounds, I've only read about 4 of them.

   So long as you don't take up smoking and wearing capes with a dollar
   sign pin on your collar, and citing the "refutations of the
   non-exclusionary corrolaries," there's little danger in Rand.

At least part of the danger in Rand is the same as the danger in any
other meme-set -- that of accepting it unconsciously and without
question.  This danger is more profound, as I stated before, with
meme-sets that espouse absolutes.

   -Tim, Unreconstructed Rand Supporter (but who refuses to believe that
   she had all the answers--an absurd idea--and is much more of an
   anarchocapitalist than she was prepared to be)

Thanks for pointing out another hitch I picked up -- that one must
support something either wholeheartedly or not at all.  It's actually
corollary to the absolutism, but deserves its own note because it's
more directly related to behavior.

Kennita

