  The wave function is not considered to be "real" in QM. The N-different
QM interpretations each assume different entities. It's certainly
possible that there are a large set of theories all isomorphic to each
other but which use different models. The facts of the matter are, we don't
know what an electron is. Is it a particle, a wave, a loop, ...?
We can only say that under some operations it acts like a particle and
under others it acts like a wave. What's important is how an electron acts,
not what it fundamentally is. 

> It is difficult to explain the rapid acceptance of general
> and special relativity from a purely operational
> standpoint.  Similarly it is impossible to explain how the
> Copernican revolution preceded Newton's theory of gravity
> from within the framework of operationalism.

  Sure it is. The scientific method as taught in grade school science is
1) recogonize and formulate a problem
2) collect data through observation and experiment
3) formulate and test a hypothesis

  Anything else is not the scientific method, I don't care what 
philosophical BS "spin" you put on it.

  The problem special relativity solved was recognized and formulated.
First hypothesis (ether, tested and failed), Einstein's hypothesis
fit the data and passed every test to date.

  Ditto QM.

  General Relativity is hard to account for since an experimental test
of it is hard to perform. However, it only assumes one additional
postulate, everything else follows. 

  If the postulates aren't too unreasonable or testable, the theory that
logically follows is strengthened.

